nt on P\u2082>P\u2081 before sending the marker have been delivered to P\u2081. The marker from P\u2082 on P\u2082>P\u2081 was sent by P\u2082 after P\u2082 recorded its local state (event 8). So P\u2082 sent the marker after event 8.\n\nAny message P\u2082 sent on P\u2082>P\u2081 before event 8 would have been sent before the marker, and since the channel is FIFO, it would have been delivered before the marker arrives at P\u2081.\n\nm\u2081 was sent by P\u2082 at event 1 (before event 8). So m\u2081 was sent before the marker from P\u2082. Since the channel is FIFO, m\u2081 arrives at P\u2081 before the marker from P\u2082. Indeed, m\u2081 arrives at event 10, and the marker arrives at event 16.\n\nSo when P\u2081 receives the marker from P\u2082, m\u2081 has already been delivered. The channel P\u2082>P\u2081 is empty at the time of the marker (no messages between m\u2081 and the marker).\n\nNow, in the snapshot:\n- P\u2081's local state is at event 5.\n- m\u2081 is received at event 10 (after P\u2081's snapshot).\n- m\u2081 is sent at event 1 (before P\u2082's snapshot at event 8).\n\nSo m\u2081 is \"in transit\" in the sense that it was sent before the sender's cut but received after the receiver's cut. But the algorithm records the channel as empty.\n\nHmm, this seems like a contradiction. But actually, I think the resolution is that the Chandy-Lamport algorithm's snapshot is NOT just a consistent cut - it's a specific representation where the \"channel state\" captures the in-transit messages in a way that's consistent with the cut.\n\nWait, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is: in the Chandy-Lamport algorithm, the channel state recorded IS the set of in-transit messages. The algorithm guarantees this. So if the algorithm says P\u2082>P\u2081 is empty, then m\u2081 is NOT in transit in the snapshot's view.\n\nBut how? m\u2081 was sent before P\u2082's snapshot and received after P\u2081's snapshot. That's the definition of in-transit.\n\nLet me reconsider the timeline more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that I need to reconsider what \"in transit\" means in the context of the cut.\n\nThe cut is:\n- P\u2081's cut point: between event 3 and event 5 (at P\u2081)\n- P\u2082's cut point: between event 6 and event 8 (at P\u2082)  \n- P\u2083's cut point: between event 7 and event 9 (at P\u2083)\n\nA message is \"in transit\" in the cut if its send is on the \"before\" side of the sender's cut and its receive is on the \"after\" side of the receiver's cut.\n\nm\u2081: send at event 1 (before P\u2082's cut), receive at event 10 (after P\u2081's cut). So m\u2081 IS in transit.\n\nBut the algorithm records P\u2082>P\u2081 as empty. This is a contradiction.\n\nUnless... I'm wrong about which channel is the \"first marker\" channel for P\u2081.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. For the initiator P\u2081:\n- P\u2081 sends markers on P\u2081>P\u2082 and P\u2081>P\u2083.\n- P\u2081 receives markers on P\u2082>P\u2081 and P\u2083>P\u2081.\n\nWhen P\u2081 receives the first marker (from P\u2082 at event 16), it treats P\u2082>P\u2081 as empty.\n\nBut m\u2081 was already received at event 10, before the marker arrived at event 16. So m\u2081 was delivered before the marker. From the algorithm's perspective, m\u2081 is NOT in the channel state because it was delivered before the marker crossed.\n\nI think the resolution is: the Chandy-Lamport algorithm's \"channel state\" is not the same as the set of in-transit messages in the cut-theoretic sense. Rather, it's a representation that, combined with the local states, gives a consistent state of the system.\n\nActually no, I think I'm wrong. Let me look at this more carefully.\n\nThe Chandy-Lamport algorithm produces a consistent global state. The global state consists of:\n1. Local states of all processes\n2. Channel states (messages in transit on each channel)\n\nThe claim is that this global state is consistent, meaning there